WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.338
The address side has the same
structure as the name side.

2
00:00:03.338 --> 00:00:05.760
AWS VPCs, Azure virtual networks,

3
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.965
Google Cloud VPC networks and NSX
segments each allocate on their own,

4
00:00:10.965 --> 00:00:15.363
so a CIDR created in a console
can overlap on-premises space,

5
00:00:15.363 --> 00:00:19.670
another cloud or another
division, and the enterprise DDI

6
00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:24.337
system doesn't know until discovery
runs or a conflict appears.

7
00:00:24.337 --> 00:00:26.262
Integration depth differs.

8
00:00:26.262 --> 00:00:31.468
Amazon VPC IPAM can hand a private
scope's authority to an external IPAM;

9
00:00:31.468 --> 00:00:34.938
since November 2025 the
book's example is Infoblox,

10
00:00:34.938 --> 00:00:38.987
which chooses a block or accepts
or rejects a requested one.

11
00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:43.009
It needs the Advanced Tier, covers
private scopes only, and isn't

12
00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:47.649
available in GovCloud or China, so it
doesn't help a GovCloud estate today.

13
00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:53.334
Azure Virtual Network Manager offers
hierarchical pools, a root and up to

14
00:00:53.334 --> 00:00:58.738
seven layers, IPv4 and IPv6, with
static CIDRs for ranges outside Azure.

15
00:00:58.738 --> 00:01:01.962
Google Cloud's internal
ranges can reserve blocks.

16
00:01:01.962 --> 00:01:04.998
Neither hands authority
to an external IPAM,

17
00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:09.124
so mirror the enterprise reservations
into them and reconcile.

18
00:01:09.124 --> 00:01:15.576
VMware Cloud Foundation 9.1 added native
Infoblox allocation and FQDN registration;

19
00:01:15.576 --> 00:01:21.300
the source is a vendor blog without IPv6
or limits, so confirm the scope first.

20
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:26.752
The rule: reserve blocks in the enterprise
IPAM before any cloud or NSX network is

21
00:01:26.752 --> 00:01:28.886
created, integrate where you can,

22
00:01:28.886 --> 00:01:32.837
mirror and reconcile where you
can't, and monitor sync health.

23
00:01:32.837 --> 00:01:37.590
Federation plus active and passive
discovery produce one hierarchy,

24
00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:43.326
which catches overlap before two networks
are peered and reports the CM-8 inventory.

25
00:01:43.326 --> 00:01:49.213
The program also wants IPAM to track
FQDNs, wildcards and bound certificates.

26
00:01:49.213 --> 00:01:54.676
IPv6: M-21-07's milestones have passed.

27
00:01:54.676 --> 00:02:01.130
Plan both protocols together; the Private
Resolver can't use IPv6-enabled subnets.
